<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1024–<a href="/years/1125">1125</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Salic dynasty, Salier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salian dynasty was a royal dynasty of the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> that provided four kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1024 to 1125, all of them crowned Holy Roman emperors. After the death of the last Ottonian emperor in 1024 the crown passed to Conrad II, who was followed by Henry III, Henry IV and Henry V. Modern historians suppose the family descended from the Widonids, nobles of Rhenish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>. The Salians made their monarchy a major European power and developed an administrative system based on public officials answerable to the crown.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Following the death of Pope John XV, the German king Otto III chose his cousin Bruno of Carinthia, a member of the <b>Salian dynasty</b>, as pope.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-3">3 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
